# ADR-003: Using DynamoDB for Blog Comments

## Status: Accepted

This decision was accepted on 2026-10-22 to provide a simple, scalable, and low-maintenance solution for storing user comments on blog posts deployed using Astro SSR on AWS.

## Context

The blog requires a comments feature that allows visitors to submit comments against individual posts without creating user accounts.

The application is deployed on AWS using Astro running in ECS, which means server-side API routes are available for processing comment submissions. Comment data must be stored persistently and remain available across container restarts and deployments.

The chosen solution should:

- Support comment creation and retrieval through API routes.
- Scale automatically with varying traffic levels.
- Minimize operational overhead.
- Integrate easily with existing AWS infrastructure.
- Avoid managing database servers.

## Decision

Comment data will be stored in Amazon DynamoDB.

Astro API routes will be responsible for:

- Accepting comment submissions.
- Validating user input.
- Writing comments to DynamoDB.
- Retrieving comments for display on blog posts.

Each comment item will contain:

- Comment ID
- Blog Post Slug
- User Name
- Comment Text
- Creation Timestamp

The table will use the blog post slug as its partition key to allow efficient retrieval of all comments associated with a specific post.

## Consequences

- Positive:
  - Fully managed database service with no server administration.
  - Automatic scaling based on workload.
  - High availability within AWS.
  - Simple integration with ECS-hosted Astro API routes.
  - Pay-for-use pricing aligns well with expected low comment volumes.
  - Supports future features such as moderation, editing, or reactions.

- Negative:
  - Introduces an additional AWS service to manage.
  - Requires IAM permissions for API access.
  - More complex than storing comments in static files.
  - DynamoDB query patterns must be considered when designing the data model.

<br><br>

## Current Implementation

The blog stores comments in DynamoDB through server-side Astro API routes running in ECS.

Users can submit a display name and comment text for a specific blog post. Comments are persisted in DynamoDB and retrieved dynamically when a post is viewed.

This approach provides persistent comment storage while maintaining a largely serverless and low-maintenance architecture within the AWS ecosystem.